---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy: Sajilo"
  description="Sajilo stores your data on your device. There is no account. An anonymous daily count of which features are used can be switched off."
  path="/privacy.html"
>
  <main class="wrap policy" id="main">
    <h1 class="display policy-h1">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Sajilo is a menu-bar app with no account. What you write stays on your computer. This page is
      the whole policy.
    </p>
    <p class="policy-updated">Last updated 26 September 2026: the daily count now includes which features were used (see below).</p>

    <section class="policy-block">
      <h2>On your device</h2>
      <p>
        Day plans, Keeper reminders and documents, photos you attach, watchlists, settings, and
        backups you export live in a database on your computer. Sajilo does not upload them. There
        is no sign-in and no cloud copy of that data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-block">
      <h2>Anonymous daily count</h2>
      <p>
        To know how many people use the app, and which parts of it are worth improving, Sajilo can
        send at most one count per UTC day. It is on by default. Switching it off in Settings › System › Privacy stops it; nothing is sent
        after that.
      </p>
      <p>Each count includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the app version, and the previous version when you have just updated</li>
        <li>operating system and CPU architecture</li>
        <li>the UTC day, and days since the last count (capped at 45)</li>
        <li>
          a random id the app generates for itself, so that one person opening Sajilo for a month
          is not counted as thirty new people
        </li>
        <li>
          from version 0.1.29, how many times each feature was used since the last count: which
          screens and tabs were opened (for example News, or Bazar › Gold &amp; Silver), and a few
          actions such as converting a date, playing a radio station, picking a rashi, opening a
          headline, saving a plan or a Keeper item, and taking or skipping a break
        </li>
        <li>
          from version 0.1.29, a few display choices: language, digits, theme, text size, reminder
          style, and which modules are switched on
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Features are counted by name only, from a fixed list in the app’s source code. The count
        never includes what you type or search for, which story you read, which station you play,
        which rashi you picked, what you save, dates, places, amounts, or anything else you enter.
        While the count is switched off, nothing is counted even on your computer, and switching it
        off deletes any counts not yet sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        That id is a random number. It is not derived from your device, your name, your network or
        anything else about you, and it is not shared with anyone. Switching the count off in
        Settings › System › Privacy stops anything being sent at all; the id stays on your computer
        unused, so turning the count back on later does not make you look like a new person.
        Restoring a backup onto another computer generates a new id rather than reusing the one from
        the computer the backup came from.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside the id, the service records the day each count was for and the time it arrived, so
        it can tell an install still in use from one that stopped. The count is sent by Sajilo’s
        hourly background refresh, not by anything you do, so that time says the app was running —
        not when you opened a screen. Feature counts are totals since the last count, with no times
        attached. There is at most one record per day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cloudflare derives an approximate country from the connection. The service does not store an
        account, your name, an email address, an IP address, or anything identifying your device.
        What you search for, read, play and save is never sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-block">
      <h2>Public sources</h2>
      <p>
        News, weather, markets, forex, rashifal, radio and similar screens fetch public pages and
        keyless APIs. Weather uses the city you picked, not GPS. Those requests go to the publisher,
        not to Sajilo. Failed or stale fetches stay on the device and are labelled as such.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-block">
      <h2>Notifications</h2>
      <p>
        Festival, holiday, IPO and Keeper reminders use the operating system’s notification
        permission. Sajilo asks for it when you turn those reminders on. The text of a reminder is
        not sent to Sajilo.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-block">
      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        Download links on sajilo.fyi go through a counted redirect, then to GitHub. The count is a
        total per hour, platform, approximate country, and referring site. There is no row per
        person: no address, user agent, or identifier.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-block">
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: <a href="mailto:contact@sajilo.fyi">contact@sajilo.fyi</a>.
        The app is open source at
        <a href="https://github.com/adarshaacharya/sajilo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .policy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    padding-top: clamp(40px, 7vw, 90px);
    padding-bottom: 40px;
    max-width: 680px;
  }
  .policy-h1 {
    font-size: clamp(48px, 8vw, 84px);
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }
  .policy-updated {
    margin-top: 18px;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--paper-3);
  }
  .policy-block {
    margin-top: 40px;
    padding-top: 28px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .policy-block h2 {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 28px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .policy-block p,
  .policy-block li {
    color: var(--paper-2);
    font-size: 17px;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  .policy-block p + p,
  .policy-block ul {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
  .policy-block ul {
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
  }
  .policy-block a {
    color: var(--gilt);
  }
  .policy-block a:hover {
    color: var(--gilt-2);
  }
</style>
